  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 331 AM CDT Sun Jun 7 2026\n\nToday through tonight: A shortwave trough crossing the Southern \nplains to Mid MS Valley will continue to bring very humid \nsoutherly flow into the forecast area today. While the trough will\nbe weakening and height falls into our forecast area negligible,\nvery moist air combined with daytime heating will support\nconsiderable coverage of showers and storms. Given the very moist\nand precip-efficient air, we can expect locally heavy rainfall, \nespecially over north central into eastern portions of the area \nwhere the lingering ascent and moisture convergence should be \ngreatest. Overall, the heavy rain and flash flood threat remains\ntoo low to introduce formal messaging in the HWO graphics, but\nwill monitor trends in this regard. \n\nGoing through the work week into next weekend: Rain chances will \ndiminish early this week as the trough moves out and is replaced \nby a strengthening subtropical ridge. The wet vegetative state \nfrom recent above normal rainfall will support very humid boundary\nlayer conditions, and this combined with increasing temperatures \nand relatively cloud free conditions associated with the ridge \nwill bring a threat for dangerous heat. Current guidance indicates\nthat heat indices/WBGT and the overall heat risk should peak Wed-\nFri when highs are expected to reach the low/mid 90s. If these \ntrends continue then formal messaging will eventually be needed in\nthe HWO graphics. /EC/",
